CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ direct static_cast to a reference-to-array type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ forward of an array lvalue keeps the array reference type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
type mismatch
dropped the body
--
N5008 [expr.static.cast]/3 + [dcl.ref]/6: with T deduced as `const char
(&)[4]' from a string literal argument, `static_cast<T &&>(t)' in
std::forward is a cast to `const char (&)[4]' whose operand is the array
lvalue -- an identity conversion.  The cast was rejected as casting const
away ("type mismatch on static_cast"): the deduced type carried the const on
the ARRAY node (`remove_reference<const char (&)[4]>::type') while the
written reference type has it on the element, and the [expr.const.cast]/8
type-chain comparison saw the two spellings as different qualification
levels.  [basic.type.qualifier]/3: cv-qualifiers applied to an array type
attach to the element type -- an array level has no qualification of its
own.  Fixed in round 163 (cast_away_constness and reference_compatible
compare the element qualification); std::forward of a string literal into
emplace/optional/pair no longer drops its body (round-162 audit).
